<HEAD>
<TITLE>Warning! Check you computer, there is new viruses you may be infected!</TITLE>
</HEAD>
<BODY>
<FONT size=2 face="verdana,arial">
            <P><B>05/05/2004</B> 
			<P><B><FONT size=3>Worm.Win32.Sasser.b</FONT></B>
            <P><B>Sasser spreads - no email required</B> 
            <P>
            <P>Sasser, the network worm which appeared over the weekend, differs 
            radically from recent widespread worms. Unlike Bagle
            and Netsky, 
            it does not spread via email, and can therefore infect machines 
            without any action from the user.</P>
            <P>Instead, Sasser has more in common with Lovesan, 
            which caused an epidemic in summer 2003. Sasser exploits a buffer 
            overrun in Microsoft Local Security Authority Subsystem Service, and 
            will cause infected computers to display an error message and to 
            repeatedly reboot when connecting to the Internet.</P>The original 
            version of Sasser spread relatively slowly as the code contained 
            errors. However, it is estimated that between 500,000 and 1 million 
            computers running Windows 2000 and Windows XP have been infected. 
            Some businesses, such as Westpac in Australia, experienced serious 
            problems. Microsoft reports 1.5 million visits to the company's site 
            to download the free removal tool.
            <P></P>
            <P>Information about the vulnerability and the patch for the flaw 
            were <A 
            href="http://www.microsoft.com/security/security_bulletins/200404_windows.asp" 
            target=_blank>made available by Microsoft</A> on 13th April 2004. 
            The appearance of the worm just 18 days (in comparison with 
            Lovesan's 30 days) after the vulnerability was made public confirms 
            current trends in malicious software. The period between a 
            vulnerability being discovered and malicious programs which target 
            the vulnerability is becoming shorter and shorter.</P>
            <P>In comparison with Lovesan, Sasser spreads slowly, and has not 
            yet infected an overwhelming numbers of computers. However, the fact 
            that this new virus was able to spread at all shows that many 
            systems remain unpatched. A better coded virus attacking the same 
            vulnerability could potentially do huge damage to businesses all 
            over the world.</P>
			<p></p>
